{"term": "puteiro", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "debauchery_atmosphere", "concept": "debauchery", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 70, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 80, "recognition_pct_peak": 80, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Adjectival use of 'puteiro' (brothel): describes a person, event, or atmosphere as exhibiting the sexual excess, moral looseness, and general debauchery of a whorehouse.", "english_term": "whorehouse-like", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Primary form is the noun 'puteiro' (brothel, from 'puta' = whore); the adjectival use — 'festa puteira', 'ambiente puteiro' — describes something as raunchy, sexually chaotic, or morally depraved in a brothel-like way."}
{"term": "luxurioso/luxuriosa", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "lasciviousness", "concept": "lasciviousness", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "literary", "crude": 35, "technical": 20, "recognition_pct_current": 65, "recognition_pct_peak": 65, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Lustful, lascivious; describing a person or behavior as driven by strong carnal desire, typically with a moral charge.", "english_term": "lustful", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "False cognate trap: English 'luxurious' means opulent; Brazilian Portuguese 'luxurioso' retains the Latin meaning of excessive sexual appetite. Predominantly literary register; rarely used in casual speech."}
{"term": "gostoso/gostosa", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "attractiveness", "concept": "sexual_attractiveness", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 15, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 99, "recognition_pct_peak": 99, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Sexually attractive, hot; describes a person as physically desirable and pleasurable to look at or be with.", "english_term": "hot", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Primary meaning is 'tasty/delicious'; sexual sense ('hot/sexy') is a natural food metonymy and is arguably the most ubiquitous Brazilian Portuguese compliment for physical attractiveness. Extremely high-frequency in everyday speech across all age groups."}
{"term": "gostosão/gostosona", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "attractiveness", "concept": "sexual_attractiveness", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 22, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 92, "recognition_pct_peak": 92, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Augmentative of gostoso/gostosa; an intensified term for someone who is extremely sexually attractive or physically irresistible.", "english_term": "total hottie", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "The augmentative suffix '-ão/-ona' intensifies 'gostoso' to mean 'super sexy'; '-ona' for women can carry connotations of voluptuous physical largeness that are still framed positively in Brazilian culture."}
{"term": "sensual", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "sensuality", "concept": "sensuality", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "loanword", "crude": 18, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 99, "recognition_pct_peak": 99, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Sensual; describing a person, movement, or experience as relating to or gratifying the physical senses, especially in a sexually arousing way.", "english_term": "sensual", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Direct cognate from Latin via Portuguese; essentially identical in meaning and register to English 'sensual'. Used across all registers from advertising copy to literary prose."}
{"term": "apimentado/apimentada", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "risque_content", "concept": "eroticism", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "euphemism", "crude": 28, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 93, "recognition_pct_peak": 93, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Literally 'spiced/peppered'; describes content, situations, or people as sexually risqué, titillating, or erotically charged.", "english_term": "spicy", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Food metaphor euphemism for sexual heat; 'uma conversa apimentada' (a spicy conversation), 'cena apimentada' (a steamy scene). Positive or neutral in connotation; not pejorative."}
{"term": "quente", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "arousal_state", "concept": "eroticism", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 22, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 99, "recognition_pct_peak": 99, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Literally 'hot'; used to describe a person as sexually attractive or a situation/content as arousing and sexually charged.", "english_term": "hot", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Universal temperature-to-sexuality metaphor; 'estar quente' means 'to be turned on'; used freely for both people ('ela é quente') and situations ('cena quente'). Overlaps with 'gostoso' but 'quente' emphasizes active arousal and heat over aesthetic appeal."}
{"term": "safadeza", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "lewdness", "concept": "lewdness", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 65, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 93, "recognition_pct_peak": 93, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Sexual naughtiness, crudeness, or shameless lewdness; also a specific crude or raunchy act.", "english_term": "crudeness", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Noun derived from 'safado/safada' (crude, naughty, shameless); denotes either the abstract quality of sexual shamelessness or a concrete naughty act ('que safadeza!' = 'what a filthy thing to do!'). Broadly celebrated rather than condemned in Brazilian popular culture."}
{"term": "tesudeza", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "horniness", "concept": "sexual_arousal", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 72, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 82, "recognition_pct_peak": 82, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The state of intense sexual arousal or horniness; also the quality of being highly sexually exciting or attractive.", "english_term": "horniness", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Derived from 'tesão' (crude slang for sexual arousal/horniness); 'tesudeza' is the noun of quality — either the state of being turned on ('que tesudeza que eu tô') or describing a person or situation as intensely arousing."}
{"term": "malicioso/maliciosa", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "sexual_suggestiveness", "concept": "salacious", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 30, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 92, "recognition_pct_peak": 92, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Sexually suggestive, naughtily knowing, or dirty-minded; describes a manner, look, or remark that implies sexual intent beneath a surface innocence.", "english_term": "naughty", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "In a sexual context specifically describes the quality of sly, knowing sexual innuendo — 'olhar malicioso' (a suggestive look), 'sorriso malicioso' (a knowing smile). Less about explicit content and more about implying sexual intent."}
{"term": "frígido/frígida", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "frigidity", "concept": "sexual_frigidity", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 15, "technical": 50, "recognition_pct_current": 80, "recognition_pct_peak": 90, "peak_year": 1975, "regional": null, "definition": "Frigid; lacking sexual desire or responsiveness; applied predominantly to women in clinical and popular discourse.", "english_term": "frigid", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Direct cognate of English 'frigid'; historically over-applied to women as a catch-all diagnosis; now considered dated and sexist. Peak usage in mid-20th-century Brazilian psychoanalytic discourse; fading from everyday speech."}
{"term": "frio/fria", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "frigidity", "concept": "sexual_frigidity", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 10, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 99, "recognition_pct_peak": 99, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Literally 'cold'; used metaphorically to describe a person as sexually unresponsive or lacking warmth and desire during intimacy.", "english_term": "cold", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Secondary sexual sense relying on the universal cold/heat responsiveness metaphor; 'ela é fria na cama' (she's cold in bed). Less clinical than 'frígido'; often simply means sexually disengaged rather than diagnosing a clinical condition."}
{"term": "inibido/inibida", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "inhibition", "concept": "sexual_inhibition", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 5, "technical": 42, "recognition_pct_current": 90, "recognition_pct_peak": 90, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Sexually inhibited; tense and unable to relax or freely express sexuality due to psychological or social constraints.", "english_term": "inhibited", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Direct cognate of English 'inhibited'; used across clinical psychology and everyday speech without vulgarity; commonly contrasted with 'desinibido/desinibida' (uninhibited)."}
{"term": "recalcado/recalcada", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "repression", "concept": "sexual_repression", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 12, "technical": 28, "recognition_pct_current": 88, "recognition_pct_peak": 88, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Sexually repressed; one whose sexual desires are suppressed or denied expression, typically due to cultural, religious, or psychological forces.", "english_term": "repressed", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Derived from the Freudian 'recalque' (repression); widely popularized in Brazilian popular speech through psychoanalytic culture; stronger and more pathologizing than 'inibido'; often used semi-humorously ('você é um recalcado!')."}
{"term": "careta", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "sexual_conservatism", "concept": "sexual_conservatism", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 10, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 93, "recognition_pct_peak": 93, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Sexually square or uptight; a person or attitude that is conventionally conservative, unwilling to sexually experiment, or easily shocked by sexual content.", "english_term": "square", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Primary meaning is 'grimace' or colloquially 'a boring/straight-laced person'; in sexual contexts means prudish or adventurously avoidant; also used in drug culture for someone who doesn't use drugs — the sexual meaning is not its only sense."}
{"term": "trancado/trancada", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "inhibition", "concept": "sexual_inhibition", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 12, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 85, "recognition_pct_peak": 85, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Literally 'locked'; describes a person as completely sexually blocked, unable to let go or express themselves sexually due to deep inhibition.", "english_term": "locked up", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "The locked-door metaphor extended to sexual personality; implies total inability to express sexuality rather than mere reluctance; stronger than 'inibido'; 'ela é trancada sexualmente' = 'she's totally locked up sexually'."}
{"term": "pudico/pudica", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "prudishness", "concept": "prudishness", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "literary", "crude": 5, "technical": 18, "recognition_pct_current": 43, "recognition_pct_peak": 55, "peak_year": 1960, "regional": null, "definition": "Modest, chaste, or prudish; excessively protective of sexual propriety or easily offended by sexual content.", "english_term": "prudish", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Formal/literary register; cognate of Latin 'pudicus' (modest, chaste); rarely used in contemporary everyday Brazilian Portuguese; understood by educated speakers but largely displaced by colloquial alternatives like 'careta' or 'moralista'."}
{"term": "assexuado/assexuada", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "asexuality", "concept": "asexuality", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 5, "technical": 32, "recognition_pct_current": 58, "recognition_pct_peak": 58, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Sexless or de-sexualized; lacking sexual characteristics, sexual desire, or sexual identity; applied to persons and to things.", "english_term": "sexless", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Distinct from 'assexual' (asexual as an identity label); 'assexuado' means descriptively 'without sexuality' and can modify anything lacking a sexual dimension. The identity term in Brazilian Portuguese is 'assexual'; 'assexuado' is descriptive rather than a claimed identity."}
{"term": "castrado/castrada", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "emasculation", "concept": "emasculation", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 48, "technical": 18, "recognition_pct_current": 88, "recognition_pct_peak": 88, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Metaphorically castrated; describing a person as stripped of sexual desire, vitality, or masculine power — rendered sexually or socially neutered.", "english_term": "neutered", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Literal meaning is 'castrated (animal or person)'; the metaphorical sexual use means emasculated, without libido, or lacking sexual courage or drive; used hyperbolically in Brazilian popular speech ('você é um castrado' = 'you have no balls/drive'). Parenthetical '(metaphorically)' in the source list signals this non-literal sense."}
{"term": "pau-mole", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "impotence", "concept": "erectile_dysfunction", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 68, "technical": 8, "recognition_pct_current": 82, "recognition_pct_peak": 82, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Literally 'soft cock'; describes a man as impotent or sexually incapable of achieving an erection; also used more broadly for cowardice or weakness.", "english_term": "limp dick", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "'Pau' is standard crude slang for penis in Brazilian Portuguese; 'mole' means soft/limp; the compound is one of the most common crude terms for male impotence. Extended metaphorically to general spinelessness ('não seja pau-mole' = 'don't be spineless')."}
{"term": "moleza", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "flaccidity", "concept": "penile_flaccidity", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 50, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 88, "recognition_pct_peak": 88, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Softness, limpness; in sexual context refers to penile flaccidity, inability to sustain erection, or broad sexual passivity and laziness.", "english_term": "limpness", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Primary meaning is general 'softness, flabbiness, laziness'; the sexual meaning exploits the same literal quality applied to the penis; can also describe overall sexual passivity in either sex ('ela/ele é uma moleza na cama' = she/he is limp/lazy in bed). Distinct from 'pau-mole' in that 'moleza' can be gender-neutral."}
{"term": "frouxo/frouxa", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "low_libido", "concept": "low_libido", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 28, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 88, "recognition_pct_peak": 88, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Literally 'loose/slack'; in sexual contexts means sexually weak, unenthusiastic, or lacking libido; also implies sexual cowardice or unwillingness to engage.", "english_term": "sexually limp", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Primary meaning is 'loose, slack, cowardly'; in sexual contexts implies low energy, low drive, or lack of passion; overlaps with 'moleza' but 'frouxo' foregrounds weakness/cowardice while 'moleza' foregrounds softness/laziness."}
{"term": "seco/seca", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "frigidity", "concept": "sexual_frigidity", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 18, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 99, "recognition_pct_peak": 99, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Literally 'dry'; used to describe sexual unresponsiveness, especially lack of vaginal lubrication in women; also used colloquially to mean 'not getting any sexual action'.", "english_term": "dry", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Exploits the dryness/wetness antonym for arousal ('molhada' = wet = aroused; 'seca' = dry = unresponsive); dual meaning: physically unlubricated AND more broadly sexually inactive or frustrated ('tô seco/a' = I'm not getting any). Context disambiguates."}
{"term": "homossexual", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "identities_orientations", "subcategory": "homosexuality", "concept": "homosexual", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 5, "technical": 68, "recognition_pct_current": 99, "recognition_pct_peak": 99, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Homosexual; a person sexually and/or romantically attracted to people of the same sex.", "english_term": "homosexual", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Direct cognate; the clinical/formal term in Brazilian Portuguese; in everyday speech largely displaced by 'gay' (men) and 'lésbica' (women); 'homossexual' reads as institutional and can feel distancing or pathologizing in community contexts."}
{"term": "heterossexual", "lang": "pt-BR", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "identities_orientations", "subcategory": "heterosexuality", "concept": "heterosexual", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 0, "technical": 68, "recognition_pct_current": 99, "recognition_pct_peak": 99, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Heterosexual; a person sexually and/or romantically attracted to people of a different sex.", "english_term": "heterosexual", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Direct cognate; the formal clinical term; informally often shortened to 'hétero' in casual Brazilian Portuguese speech; the full form is most common in academic, medical, or institutional settings."}
